course: everyday
lesson: 270
title: Trains, the Underground and subways
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Railway tracks are never a place for people: trains come fast and cannot stop quickly, and many lines carry deadly electricity in a third rail beside the running rails.
explain: So if you drop something onto the tracks, you never climb down; you tell a member of staff, who has safe ways to get it.
explain: Stations are big but logical: in London you follow the line's name and colour and the last station in your direction, and on escalators you stand on the right so people in a hurry can walk on the left.
explain: Before a trip, a family in New York agrees a plan: if they get separated, whoever is left on the train gets off at the next station and waits on the platform for the others.
fact: If something falls onto the railway tracks, you never climb down; you tell a member of staff.
fact: The third rail carries enough electricity to kill, so you never go near the tracks.
fact: You step carefully over the gap between the train and the platform, because a foot can slip into it.
fact: A family makes a plan before travelling in case they get separated, such as getting off at the next station and waiting on the platform, or staying on and telling staff.
fact: You buy a ticket before boarding or use the right card, and keep it for checking, because travelling without one can mean a fine.
fact: On the London Underground you find your way by the line's name and colour and the last station in your direction.
fact: On London escalators people stand on the right and walk on the left.
fact: On an escalator you hold the handrail, keep shoes and laces away from the sides, and step off at the end.
fact: In a crowded carriage you take off your backpack and hold it in front of you or at your feet, which gives others more space and stops it bumping them.
fact: You do not touch an unattended bag on a platform; you tell staff, who will check it.
fact: People with prams, wheelchairs and luggage go first in a station lift, because they cannot use stairs or escalators.
exercise: Your phone falls onto the tracks at the station. What do you do? | Tell a member of staff and never climb down | Climb down quickly before a train comes | Use your umbrella to reach it from the edge | Ask a friend to hold your hand while you climb down
exercise: Why must you never go down onto the tracks? | Trains come fast and the rails may be electrified | You might scratch your shoes on the stones | Staff do not like people on the tracks | The tracks are too slippery
exercise: What is the third rail? | A rail carrying enough electricity to kill | A spare rail for when one breaks | A rail for children to hold on the platform | A rail to stop trains sliding in the rain
exercise: The train doors open and there is a gap between the train and the platform. What do you do? | Step over it carefully and mind the gap | Jump across the gap as far as you can go | Put one foot in the gap first to test it | Wait for the doors to close and then step
exercise: Before a train trip, what should a family agree? | A plan for what to do if they get separated | Which seat each person will sit in on every train they take | How many snacks each person may eat | Who will hold the tickets
exercise: You are on the train and the doors close with your dad still on the platform. What is a good plan? | Get off at the next station and wait there, or tell staff | Pull the emergency handle and get off at once | Stay on the train to the end of the line and wait for him there | Ask a stranger to take you home
exercise: Why should you keep your train ticket after you board? | It may be checked, and without one you can be fined | It is a nice souvenir of the trip | You need it to open the toilet door on the train | The driver collects them
exercise: How do you find the right train on the London Tube? | Follow the line's colour and the last station in your direction | Get on the first train that comes | Follow the people who look like they know where they are going | Choose the emptiest platform
exercise: On which side do people stand on London Underground escalators? | On the right, so others can walk on the left | On the left, so others can walk on the right | In the middle, holding both rails | Anywhere they like
exercise: How do you ride an escalator safely? | Hold the rail and keep shoes and laces away from the sides | Sit down on the steps to rest your legs on the way up | Run up the moving steps for fun | Lean over the side to look down
exercise: Why should you keep your laces away from the sides of an escalator? | They can get caught in the moving parts | They make the escalator go slower for everyone behind you | The sides are wet and dirty | The escalator might stop
exercise: The subway car is crowded and you wear a big backpack. What do you do? | Take it off and hold it in front or at your feet | Keep it on and turn around often to see who it bumps | Put it on an empty seat beside you | Push through the crowd with it on
exercise: You see a bag left alone on a platform. What do you do? | Do not touch it; tell staff | Open it to find the owner's name | Carry it to the lost property office | Kick it away from the platform edge
exercise: The station lift arrives and a woman with a pram is waiting too. What do you do? | Let her go first, as she cannot use stairs or escalators | Get in first, because you were there first | Squeeze in beside the pram | Hold the door shut until it is your turn, then let her have the next one
test: A girl in Toronto sees her hat blow onto the subway tracks. Which is the right choice? | Ask a staff member to get it for her | Climb down while no train is in sight | Lie flat on the platform and reach down for it | Jump down and climb back up very quickly
test: Why is a train harder to dodge than a car? | It is fast, heavy and cannot stop or turn quickly | It always rings a bell before it comes | It moves slower than a car | It can steer away from people
test: A boy in London needs the Central line to Epping. How does he find the right platform? | Follow the red Central line signs that say Epping | Get on any red train that comes into any station | Take whichever train arrives first | Follow the signs to the exit
test: An escalator at a London station has a crowd standing on both sides. What will people in a hurry say? | Please stand on the right | Please walk on the right-hand side | Please sit down on the steps | Please run faster up the left
test: A man leaves the train without paying because the gate was open. What can happen? | He can be fined if his ticket is checked | Nothing, because only the gates matter | He can pay next time instead, when he comes back | The train will refuse to start
test: Which family is best prepared for a big city trip? | The one that agreed where to meet if anyone gets lost | The one that brought the most snacks and drinks for the long day | The one that will walk very fast | The one that will not talk
test: A girl's sandal gets close to the yellow edge of an escalator step. Why should she move it? | The sides can catch shoes and laces | The yellow line is wet paint that will stain her sandal | The escalator will speed up | She will be asked to get off
test: A boy finds a rucksack on a bench at the station with nobody near. What should he do? | Tell a member of staff and leave it alone | Look inside for something with the owner's name on it | Take it home and wait for the owner | Throw it in the bin
test: Why do people take off their backpacks on a crowded train? | It gives others more space | Backpacks are not allowed on trains | It makes the train lighter | The train is too warm
test: dropped my phone on the tracks!!! | Tell staff, never climb down | Jump down quickly before a train | Lie down flat and reach for it carefully | Climb down when no train is in sight
test: someone's bag left alone on the platform, should i look inside | No, don't touch it, tell staff | Yes, find the owner's name inside | Carry it to the ticket office yourself so it's safe | Kick it to see if it's heavy or empty
test: train door closing n mum's still on the platform!! | Get off at the next station and wait, as you planned | Pull the doors open | Jump out as it moves | Get off anywhere and walk home
test: packed train, my backpack keeps hitting ppl | Take it off and hold it in front or at your feet | Turn round so it hits others | Keep it on and say sorry each time it bumps someone | Wear it higher on your back
explain: A teenager in New York climbed down onto the subway tracks to get his dropped phone; he touched the third rail and was badly burned by the electricity.
explain: A small boy in London had his foot slip into the gap between the train and the platform; staff stopped the train and freed him, but his leg was bruised and he was very frightened.
fact: The electricity in a third rail or overhead wire can kill a person in a moment, and it is always switched on.
fact: A train needs hundreds of metres to stop, so a driver who sees someone on the tracks usually cannot stop in time.
test: dropped my headphones on the tracks!! can i just hop down quick | No, tell a staff member, they'll get them safely | Yes, if no train is coming | Yes, but avoid the shiny rail | Yes, a friend can stand at the edge and watch for trains for you
test: whats the big deal with the third rail | It carries electricity strong enough to kill | It is only a little warm to the touch | It only works at night | It is there to hold the trains up on the track
test: kid fell in the gap at the station, what do u do | Shout for staff and the driver so the train doesn't move | Pull the child out as hard as you can | Get on the train and tell no one | Push the train doors closed so the train can leave the station quickly
test: omg someone at school said a man climbed down for his hat on the tube tracks and got zapped, "like in a film, sparks everywhere". real lesson? | Never go down on the tracks; tell a member of staff | Only grab things when no train is coming | Hats should be tied on in the Underground | The rails are only live at rush hour, so go down early in the morning
test: my brother reckons a driver saw a kid on the line and stopped "like 2 inches away". what's prob exaggerated | The close stop, since trains need hundreds of metres to stop | That trains run on rails at all | That drivers look out at the track | That there was a train on the line
test: heard a girl's foot went in the gap at the station and "they had to cut the train open". should u spread that | No, it's likely blown up; just mind the gap yourself | Yes, it's a great story to tell everyone | Yes, and add her name so it sounds real | Yes, but say you saw it yourself, people believe it more
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate response when a personal article falls upon the railway tracks? | The article shall be reported to staff and no descent attempted | The article may be retrieved provided no train is visible | The article may be recovered by lying flat upon the platform edge | The article shall be abandoned and the matter concealed
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the conductor rail, hereinafter the third rail, must at all times be avoided. | It carries electricity sufficient to cause death | It is heated to a dangerous temperature in winter months | It is energised solely during peak hours | It is liable to collapse under a person's weight
exam: It is given that a family travelling by rail becomes separated. Select the response consistent with sound prior arrangement. | A plan agreed beforehand, such as alighting at the next station and waiting | Each member shall proceed independently to the final destination | The child shall remain aboard until the line terminates and tell nobody | The child shall leave the station in search of the family
exam: State the conduct expected of a passenger upon discovering an unattended bag upon a platform. | The passenger shall not touch it and shall inform staff | The passenger shall open it to ascertain its owner | The passenger shall carry it to the lost property office | The passenger shall move it clear of the platform edge
